Implement Firestore Security Rules and verify them carefully.

Rules must enforce:
- Customers can access only their own protected information.
- Customers cannot change their role.
- Customers cannot write trusted booking or payment status fields directly.
- Customers cannot write slot locks directly.
- Only authorized owners/admins can perform management actions.
- Sensitive operations must execute on the trusted server.

Implement:
- Authentication verification.
- Authorization checks.
- Input sanitization.
- Secure secrets management.
- Payment signature verification.
- Rate limiting.
- Protection against duplicate bookings.
- Safe error handling.

Use the Firebase Admin SDK only in trusted server-side code.

Do not place service account credentials or payment secrets inside frontend bundles.

==================================================
20. DEMONSTRATION AND INITIAL SETUP
==================================================

Create an owner onboarding process.

On first startup, the owner should be able to configure:

- Business name.
- Turf address.
- Phone number.
- Business hours.
- Currency.
- Timezone.
- Courts.
- Sport types.
- Slot durations.
- Pricing.
- Booking policies.

Provide a secure initial admin setup process.

Create a small set of clearly labelled sample courts, sample customers and sample bookings for demonstration.

The owner should be able to remove demo data before using the platform for real customers.

Do not hardcode fake users into production authentication.

Provide a clear environment variable template for all required integrations.

==================================================
21. TESTING AND QUALITY ASSURANCE
==================================================

Do not consider the project complete merely because the homepage renders.

Test the following end-to-end workflows:

TEST 1:
A customer registers, logs in, selects a date, chooses an available slot, completes a valid payment flow and receives a confirmed booking.

TEST 2:
A second customer opens the same date and sees that the previously booked slot is unavailable.

TEST 3:
The owner logs in and sees the first booking in the admin dashboard.

TEST 4:
The owner creates a walk-in booking.

TEST 5:
The customer booking page immediately reflects the walk-in reservation.

TEST 6:
Two customers attempt to reserve overlapping slots simultaneously. Only one reservation must succeed.

TEST 7:
A payment fails or a booking hold expires. The affected slot must become available again.

TEST 8:
A customer cancels an eligible booking. Availability and booking records must update correctly.

TEST 9:
An unauthorized customer attempts to open an admin route. Access must be denied.

TEST 10:
The owner filters reports and exports a CSV file.

TEST 11:
A customer refreshes the page and can still see their bookings.

TEST 12:
An online payment webhook is received more than once. The same booking must not be confirmed or charged twice.

Create automated tests for the booking engine, pricing calculation, access control and reservation conflict logic where possible.

If a test cannot be executed in the current environment, clearly explain what is required to execute it instead of claiming it passed.

